===================================================
-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
1. ORDERED OPERATIONS
Ordered operations execute sequentially. If one operation fails, MongoDB stops executing the remaining operations.

Example (Bulk Write - Ordered):
db.users.bulkWrite([
  { insertOne: { document: { name: "Nikesh", age: 25 } } },
  { insertOne: { document: { name: "Ravi", age: 30 } } },
  { updateOne: { filter: { name: "Nikesh" }, update: { $set: { age: 26 } } } }
], { ordered: true })

Explanation:
- Operations run in order.
- If one fails, subsequent operations are not executed.

------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
2. UNORDERED OPERATIONS
Unordered operations execute independently. If one operation fails, MongoDB continues with the rest.

Example (Bulk Write - Unordered):
db.users.bulkWrite([
  { insertOne: { document: { name: "Amit", age: 22 } } },
  { insertOne: { document: { name: "Sita", age: 28 } } },
  { updateOne: { filter: { name: "Amit" }, update: { $set: { age: 23 } } } }
], { ordered: false })

Explanation:
- Operations run in parallel (not strictly sequential).
- Failures do not stop other operations.
================================================================
================================================================
================================================================
In MongoDB, insertMany can run in two modes: ordered (default) and unordered. Ordered inserts stop at the first error, while unordered inserts continue inserting the remaining documents even if some fail. This difference is crucial when handling duplicate _id values or validation errors. 

🟢 Ordered Inserts ({ ordered: true })
Default behavior if you don’t specify the option.
Documents are inserted sequentially.
If one document fails (e.g., duplicate _id), MongoDB stops immediately and does not attempt the remaining documents.
Useful when you want strict control and consistency.

🔵 Unordered Inserts ({ ordered: false })
Documents are inserted in parallel (not strictly sequential).
If one document fails, MongoDB skips it and continues with the rest.
Useful when inserting large batches where some errors are acceptable.

Both modes return a result object with:
acknowledged → whether the operation was confirmed.
insertedIds → IDs of successfully inserted documents.

Unordered inserts are faster for bulk operations since they don’t stop at the first error.
Ordered inserts are safer when you need predictable, all-or-nothing behavior. 
